I've been working on a TypeScript library that allows users to use an interface to define a set of possible values, and the library will use that interface to generate a union type of all those possible values. For example, if you had an interface like this:

interface Animal {  name: string;}interface Cat extends Animal {  meow(): void;}interface Dog extends Animal {  bark(): void;}interface Bird extends Animal {  fly(): void;}interface Fish extends Animal {  swim(): void;}


Then the library would generate this type:

type Animal = Cat | Dog | Bird | Fish;


This works great when the user is defining their interface, but the problem comes when the user wants to use the generated type. For example, if they have an object of type Animal and they want to check if it's a Cat, they can't just do if (animal instanceof Cat) because TypeScript won't let them use instanceof on a union type. They could use a type guard function, like this:

function isCat(animal: Animal): animal is Cat {  return (animal as Cat).meow!== undefined;}

if (isCat(animal)) {  // Do something with the cat}


But this is a bit clunky and doesn't really read well

